## Biographies
Quan-Guo Zhai received his Ph.D. in Chemistry from Fujian Institute of Research on the Structure of Matter, Chinese Academy of Sciences in 2007 with Can-Zhong Lu. He joined Shaanxi Normal University in 2007 and became a professor in 2014. His current interest is on the design of novel MOFs for energy applications.
Xianhui Bu received his Ph.D. in Chemistry from State University of New York at Buffalo (1992), with Philip Coppens. Between 1992 and 2003, he did his research with Prof. Galen Stucky at UC Santa Barbara, while supervising the X-ray diffraction facility. He joined the faculty at California State University Long Beach in 2003. His research area includes synthesis, structure, and property characterization of CPMs.
Xiang Zhao received his Ph.D. in Chemistry from UC Riverside with Pingyun Feng in 2011 where he is currently a research associate. His interest is on the design of MOFs for energy and environmental applications.
Dong-Sheng Li received his Ph.D. in Chemistry from Northwest University of China in 2005 with Yao-Yu Wang. He joined the faculty at China Three Gorges University in 2008. His interest is on the design of MOFs for energy and environmental applications.
Pingyun Feng received her Ph.D. in Chemistry from UC Santa Barbara (1998) where she also did postdoctoral work at Department of Chemical Engineering (1998−2000). She joined the faculty at UC Riverside in 2000. Her interest centers on the development of synthetic methodologies to prepare novel materials for energy conversion and storage.
